00:17:20.460 --> 00:17:23.400
Now we focus on
selecting the resources

00:17:23.400 --> 00:17:26.250
that the DCR will apply to.

00:17:26.250 --> 00:17:31.050
For non-Azure machine, they must
be connected through Azure Arc,

00:17:31.050 --> 00:17:35.720
and when you add machine,
add DCR resources,

00:17:35.720 --> 00:17:40.160
any machine that doesn't already
have an Azure Monitoring Agent,

00:17:40.160 --> 00:17:44.420
whether it's Windows or Linux,
will have the agent installed

00:17:44.420 --> 00:17:47.910
as part of that
onboarding process.

00:17:48.990 --> 00:17:52.740
Here, we examine the DCR
configuration itself.

00:17:52.740 --> 00:17:55.470
This is where you
specify exactly

00:17:55.470 --> 00:17:58.950
which Window Security
event to collect

00:17:58.950 --> 00:18:02.850
and how they should be
delivered into your workspace,

00:18:02.850 --> 00:18:07.440
giving you centralized
control over volume, cost,

00:18:07.440 --> 00:18:10.300
and investigative value.

00:18:11.700 --> 00:18:13.440
Next, let's bring in log

00:18:13.440 --> 00:18:18.690
from external security solution
using the Common Event Format,

00:18:18.690 --> 00:18:20.150
or CAF.

00:18:20.150 --> 00:18:25.070
This is a common scenario where
you're integrating network,

00:18:25.070 --> 00:18:29.540
firewall, or third-party
security appliances data

00:18:29.540 --> 00:18:31.530
into Sentinel.

00:18:31.530 --> 00:18:33.960
After completing this
module, you'll be able

00:18:33.960 --> 00:18:38.490
to explain the CAF connector
deployment option in Sentinel,

00:18:38.490 --> 00:18:42.570
and you'll be able to explain
the difference between the CAF

00:18:42.570 --> 00:18:46.740
and Syslog connector, so you
can choose the right approach

00:18:46.740 --> 00:18:52.440
for your source system and for
the level of parsing you need.

00:18:52.440 --> 00:18:56.670
Here's the typical deployment
model for CAF ingestion.

00:18:56.670 --> 00:19:00.900
You deploy a syslog forwarder
server that acts as a bridge

00:19:00.900 --> 00:19:03.630
between your appliance
and Sentinel.

00:19:03.630 --> 00:19:06.990
That forwarder is a
dedicated Linux machine

00:19:06.990 --> 00:19:10.680
with the Azure Monitoring
Agent for Linux installed.

00:19:10.680 --> 00:19:14.790
Many vendor-specific
Sentinel connectors rely

00:19:14.790 --> 00:19:17.460
on the CAF connectors
behind the scene.

00:19:17.460 --> 00:19:22.260
You can deploy this forwarder
in Azure or on-premise,

00:19:22.260 --> 00:19:26.310
and importantly, CAF is
generally recommended

00:19:26.310 --> 00:19:32.040
over basic syslog because CAF
provides parse message data,

00:19:32.040 --> 00:19:34.800
which reduces the amount
of parsing you need

00:19:34.800 --> 00:19:39.600
to do during queries
and investigation.

00:19:39.600 --> 00:19:43.590
This architecture diagram
illustrates how CAF logs

00:19:43.590 --> 00:19:48.570
collection data using the
common event format using AMA

00:19:48.570 --> 00:19:51.540
connectors currently in preview.

00:19:51.540 --> 00:19:55.320
Your security appliance sends
syslog to the forwarder.

00:19:55.320 --> 00:19:58.590
The forwarder uses
its syslog daemon

00:19:58.590 --> 00:20:01.950
and the Azure monitoring
agent for Linux to collect

00:20:01.950 --> 00:20:05.520
and securely send those
events to Sentinel,

00:20:05.520 --> 00:20:10.380
where they're parsed and
enriched for analysis.

00:20:10.380 --> 00:20:13.590
Let's walk through the
connection process.

00:20:13.590 --> 00:20:16.800
You start by creating
a Data Collection Rule

00:20:16.800 --> 00:20:21.060
and then use the link on the
connector page to get a script.

00:20:21.060 --> 00:20:24.480
You run that script on the
designated Linux machine

00:20:24.480 --> 00:20:26.720
that will act as your forwarder.

00:20:26.720 --> 00:20:31.340
That machine can be physical
or virtual, could be on-prem,

00:20:31.340 --> 00:20:34.100
in Azure, or another cloud,

00:20:34.100 --> 00:20:37.850
as long as it can receive
the syslog traffic

00:20:37.850 --> 00:20:40.680
from your security solution.

00:20:40.680 --> 00:20:42.180
When you run the script,

00:20:42.180 --> 00:20:45.780
it installs the Azure
Monitoring Agent for Linux

00:20:45.780 --> 00:20:49.320
and configures it to
listen to CAF messages

00:20:49.320 --> 00:20:52.560
from the built-in Linux
syslog daemon running

00:20:52.560 --> 00:20:55.620
on TCP port 25226.

00:20:55.620 --> 00:21:00.420
It also configures secure
transport over TLS

00:21:00.420 --> 00:21:02.850
to your Sentinel workspace,

00:21:02.850 --> 00:21:06.510
where the messages are
parsed and enriched.

00:21:06.510 --> 00:21:10.320
At the same time, the script
configures the syslog daemon,

00:21:10.320 --> 00:21:16.800
such as rsyslog or syslog-ng,
to listen to syslog messages

00:21:16.800 --> 00:21:21.720
from your security
solution on TCP port 514

00:21:21.720 --> 00:21:26.100
and then forwards only the
messages identified as CAF

00:21:26.100 --> 00:21:32.140
to the agent locally
on TCP port 25226.

00:21:33.900 --> 00:21:38.300
Now, let's move to
syslog ingestion.

00:21:38.300 --> 00:21:43.640
While CAF is a structured format
layered on top of syslog,

00:21:43.640 --> 00:21:48.720
many environments still need to
connect to plain syslog events

00:21:48.720 --> 00:21:52.770
from Linux hosts and
appliance and then parse them

00:21:52.770 --> 00:21:55.950
as needed into Sentinel.

00:21:55.950 --> 00:21:58.560
After completing this
module, you'll be able

00:21:58.560 --> 00:22:02.970
to describe the Syslog Data
Collection Rule configuration

00:22:02.970 --> 00:22:07.650
in Microsoft Azure, understand
how the Azure Monitoring Agent

00:22:07.650 --> 00:22:14.490
for Linux is deployed, create
and configure a DCR to send data

00:22:14.490 --> 00:22:20.220
to Sentinel, and create a
parser using KQL in Sentinel.

00:22:20.220 --> 00:22:23.100
And that will reinforce
that creating

00:22:23.100 --> 00:22:26.430
and configuring the DCR
is the central step

00:22:26.430 --> 00:22:31.110
that ties collection
to your workspace.

00:22:31.110 --> 00:22:33.390
Here's the overall approach.

00:22:33.390 --> 00:22:37.380
You stream events from your
Linux-based syslog supporting

00:22:37.380 --> 00:22:39.570
machine or appliances

00:22:39.570 --> 00:22:44.190
into Sentinel using the Azure
Monitoring Agent for Linux

00:22:44.190 --> 00:22:46.650
and Data Collection Rules.

00:22:46.650 --> 00:22:50.430
The host native syslog
daemon collects local event

00:22:50.430 --> 00:22:55.230
of specified type and forwards
them locally to the agent,

00:22:55.230 --> 00:22:59.490
and the agent streams them to
your log analytic workspace.

00:22:59.490 --> 00:23:02.940
From a how it works perspective,

00:23:02.940 --> 00:23:07.170
syslog is a common Linux
event logging protocol

00:23:07.170 --> 00:23:11.430
where the Azure monitoring
agent for Linux is installed.

00:23:11.430 --> 00:23:15.930
It configures the local syslog
daemon to forward message

00:23:15.930 --> 00:23:21.000
to the agent on TCP port 28330.

00:23:21.000 --> 00:23:23.910
The agent then sends
those messages

00:23:23.910 --> 00:23:30.450
to your log analytic workspace
over TCP 443 using HTTPS.

00:23:30.450 --> 00:23:35.190
Once ingested, the events
are parsed into entries

00:23:35.190 --> 00:23:40.710
in the syslog table, which
you can query using Sentinel

00:23:40.710 --> 00:23:42.640
under logs.

00:23:44.220 --> 00:23:48.210
This diagram shows the syslog
collection architecture

00:23:48.210 --> 00:23:49.860
from a single source.

00:23:49.860 --> 00:23:52.200
The key takeaway is the flow.

00:23:52.200 --> 00:23:54.780
Source generates syslogs.

00:23:54.780 --> 00:23:59.580
The agent and local daemon
cooperate to forward events,

00:23:59.580 --> 00:24:03.090
and the workspace receives
and stores those events

00:24:03.090 --> 00:24:06.870
for Sentinel queries
and detection.

00:24:06.870 --> 00:24:11.880
Here we focus on onboarding
Azure Linux Virtual Machine.

00:24:11.880 --> 00:24:17.040
When you add Azure
Linux VM as resources

00:24:17.040 --> 00:24:19.680
in Syslog Data Collection Rule,

00:24:19.680 --> 00:24:22.920
the Azure monitoring
agent is deployed.

00:24:22.920 --> 00:24:26.730
So to connect an
Azure based Linux VM,

00:24:26.730 --> 00:24:30.540
you add it as a DCR resource,

00:24:30.540 --> 00:24:34.920
and the platform handles agent
deployment and association

00:24:34.920 --> 00:24:38.200
so events can start flowing.

00:24:40.230 --> 00:24:45.390
For non-Azure Linux host, the
approach changes slightly.

00:24:45.390 --> 00:24:48.750
You provision those
machines using Azure Arc.

00:24:48.750 --> 00:24:52.800
There are more steps involved
compared to Azure Native VM,

00:24:52.800 --> 00:24:56.220
and you generally need to
manually install the agent

00:24:56.220 --> 00:24:59.460
as part of bringing that
host under management

00:24:59.460 --> 00:25:04.800
so it can participate in
DCR-based collection.

00:25:04.800 --> 00:25:08.820
Now we configure what
actually gets collected.

00:25:08.820 --> 00:25:13.170
The Data Collection Rule
will only collect events

00:25:13.170 --> 00:25:17.820
that match the facilities
and severities you specify.

00:25:17.820 --> 00:25:21.030
This is a critical
control point.

00:25:21.030 --> 00:25:23.910
It lets you balance
investigative coverage

00:25:23.910 --> 00:25:27.930
with data volume and
cost, while ensuring

00:25:27.930 --> 00:25:32.250
that the event you do ingest
are the ones your detection

00:25:32.250 --> 00:25:35.650
and hunting workflow
actually need.

00:25:37.380 --> 00:25:42.510
Let's dive deeper into how
syslog data is parsed once

00:25:42.510 --> 00:25:43.950
it's ingested.

00:25:43.950 --> 00:25:48.330
The syslog connector writes
events into the syslog table,

00:25:48.330 --> 00:25:53.040
and unlike the CAF connector,
the message payload is stored

00:25:53.040 --> 00:25:57.690
as a single string in the
syslog message field.

00:25:57.690 --> 00:26:02.400
That means parsing is something
you must do manually,

00:26:02.400 --> 00:26:04.080
you must handle.

00:26:04.080 --> 00:26:06.600
One effective approach is

00:26:06.600 --> 00:26:10.470
to create a reusable
parser using KQL.

00:26:10.470 --> 00:26:15.750
In this example, a function
name MyParser is created.

00:26:15.750 --> 00:26:19.170
The query starts from
the syslog table,

00:26:19.170 --> 00:26:23.340
filters on a specific
process name such as squid,

00:26:23.340 --> 00:26:27.030
and then uses the
extract statement

00:26:27.030 --> 00:26:29.220
to pull structured field

00:26:29.220 --> 00:26:32.220
from the raw syslog
message text field

00:26:32.220 --> 00:26:38.130
like URL source IP address,
username, RemotePort,

00:26:38.130 --> 00:26:42.630
and domains are extracted
and made available

00:26:42.630 --> 00:26:45.090
as individual columns.

00:26:45.090 --> 00:26:48.660
By saving this query as a
function, you only need

00:26:48.660 --> 00:26:51.270
to write the parsing logic once.

00:26:51.270 --> 00:26:54.630
After that, you can
refer MyParser just

00:26:54.630 --> 00:26:57.780
like any other
table in Sentinel.

00:26:57.780 --> 00:27:02.580
In practice, you create the
query in the log experience,

00:27:02.580 --> 00:27:05.280
select "Save,"
choose "Function,"

00:27:05.280 --> 00:27:08.640
and then assign a
function name and alias.

00:27:08.640 --> 00:27:13.740
From that point forward, calling
MyParser gives you consistently

00:27:13.740 --> 00:27:19.110
parse syslog data without
repeating the parsing logic

00:27:19.110 --> 00:27:22.920
in every query.

00:27:22.920 --> 00:27:26.610
Next, we shift focus to
Threat Intelligence.

00:27:26.610 --> 00:27:29.580
Dissecting threat
indicator allows Sentinel

00:27:29.580 --> 00:27:36.060
to match law data against known
malicious IPs, domains, URL,

00:27:36.060 --> 00:27:39.840
and file hashes,
turning raw telemetry

00:27:39.840 --> 00:27:42.930
into actionable detection.

00:27:42.930 --> 00:27:46.320
This capability help
bridges the gap

00:27:46.320 --> 00:27:51.120
between external intelligence
and internal telemetry,

00:27:51.120 --> 00:27:53.940
enabling detection
that are informed

00:27:53.940 --> 00:27:58.920
by what's happening beyond
your own environment.

00:27:58.920 --> 00:28:01.830
After completing this
module, you'll be able

00:28:01.830 --> 00:28:04.650
to install the Threat
Intelligence Content Hub

00:28:04.650 --> 00:28:08.520
solution, configure Threat
Intelligence Connector,

00:28:08.520 --> 00:28:13.410
use analytic rules, hunting
queries, and workbook,

00:28:13.410 --> 00:28:17.280
and view threat indicators
in Sentinel logs

00:28:17.280 --> 00:28:21.090
through the Threat
Intel indicator table.

00:28:21.090 --> 00:28:25.620
These skills are foundational
for enriching detection

00:28:25.620 --> 00:28:27.510
with external context

00:28:27.510 --> 00:28:32.670
and improving
investigation efficiency.

00:28:32.670 --> 00:28:37.290
Here we outline what the Threat
Intelligence solution provides.

00:28:37.290 --> 00:28:40.050
It includes multiple
data connectors

00:28:40.050 --> 00:28:42.210
such as TAXII-based feed,

00:28:42.210 --> 00:28:46.440
the Threat Intelligence
Upload Indicator API,

00:28:46.440 --> 00:28:51.600
Microsoft Defender Threat
Intelligence, and integration

00:28:51.600 --> 00:28:55.650
with third-party Threat
Intelligence platform.

00:28:55.650 --> 00:29:00.030
Analytics rules use these
indicators to generate alerts

00:29:00.030 --> 00:29:05.790
and incidents where match
occurs in your log data.

00:29:05.790 --> 00:29:10.350
Workbook summarizes indicator
activities and trends,

00:29:10.350 --> 00:29:15.720
while hunting queries allows
analysts to proactively search

00:29:15.720 --> 00:29:17.820
for related activities.

00:29:17.820 --> 00:29:22.140
Together, these components
help operationalize Threat

00:29:22.140 --> 00:29:26.130
Intelligence within Sentinel.

00:29:26.130 --> 00:29:28.890
Installing the Threat
Intelligence solution

00:29:28.890 --> 00:29:31.260
from the Content Hub
gives you access

00:29:31.260 --> 00:29:33.720
to five built-in connectors.

00:29:33.720 --> 00:29:37.950
These include Connector for
Threat Intelligence Platform,

00:29:37.950 --> 00:29:40.830
the Upload Indicator API,

00:29:40.830 --> 00:29:44.130
Microsoft Defender
Threat Intelligence,

00:29:44.130 --> 00:29:47.700
Premium Microsoft Defender
Threat Intelligence,

00:29:47.700 --> 00:29:49.830
and TAXII feeds.

00:29:49.830 --> 00:29:53.730
Once installed, these
connectors make it possible

00:29:53.730 --> 00:29:58.710
to ingest indicators from
multiple sources into Sentinel

00:29:58.710 --> 00:30:01.800
in a standardized way.

00:30:01.800 --> 00:30:07.500
With indicators flowing in, the
next step is enabling detection.

00:30:07.500 --> 00:30:11.460
Threat Intelligence analytic
rule template allows you

00:30:11.460 --> 00:30:13.980
to create alerts and incident

00:30:13.980 --> 00:30:17.520
when log event matches
known indicator.

00:30:17.520 --> 00:30:20.820
By enabling and tuning
these templates,

00:30:20.820 --> 00:30:25.920
Sentinel can automatically
service activities that align

00:30:25.920 --> 00:30:30.480
with known malicious
infrastructure helping analysts

00:30:30.480 --> 00:30:34.920
focus on high-confidence
threats.

00:30:34.920 --> 00:30:37.080
In addition to
built-in connectors,

00:30:37.080 --> 00:30:39.900
you can also send
indicators directly

00:30:39.900 --> 00:30:44.040
to Sentinel using the
Upload Indicators API.

00:30:44.040 --> 00:30:48.690
This allows application or
Threat Intelligence platform

00:30:48.690 --> 00:30:54.030
to push indicators in Sentinel
using REST-based method.

00:30:54.030 --> 00:30:59.010
This approach is commonly used
when integrating custom tooling

00:30:59.010 --> 00:31:02.520
or centralizing Threat
Intelligence platforms

00:31:02.520 --> 00:31:07.860
that already manage
indicator lifecycle.

00:31:07.860 --> 00:31:10.740
Once indicators are ingested,

00:31:10.740 --> 00:31:13.200
they're available
in multiple places.

00:31:13.200 --> 00:31:16.950
You can view them directly in
the Threat Intelligence Blade

00:31:16.950 --> 00:31:23.830
or query them from the Threat
Intel Indicator table in Logs.

00:31:25.920 --> 00:31:29.430
At this point in the session,
everything comes together.

00:31:29.430 --> 00:31:34.260
This demonstration shows how
logs are connected to Sentinel

00:31:34.260 --> 00:31:38.730
across multiple sources,
reinforcing how connectors,

00:31:38.730 --> 00:31:40.440
Data Collection Rules,

00:31:40.440 --> 00:31:46.270
and Content Hub solution
work together in practice.

00:31:47.910 --> 00:31:51.570
In this demo, let's
start connecting data

00:31:51.570 --> 00:31:55.290
to our Sentinel using
Data Connector.

00:31:55.290 --> 00:31:59.760
We've already got our Sentinel
workspace open over here.

00:31:59.760 --> 00:32:03.700
Let's navigate to the
Content Management Hub.

00:32:04.830 --> 00:32:07.470
Let's select the Content Hub.

00:32:07.470 --> 00:32:09.570
And what we're going to do
over here is we're going

00:32:09.570 --> 00:32:12.210
to connect Microsoft
Defender for Cloud.

00:32:12.210 --> 00:32:15.300
Let's search for it,
Defender for Cloud.

00:32:15.300 --> 00:32:18.750
It's already installed
but not configured.

00:32:18.750 --> 00:32:21.220
So let's select it.

00:32:22.800 --> 00:32:25.900
And let's go down to "Manage."

00:32:30.270 --> 00:32:32.310
Here are our connectors.

00:32:32.310 --> 00:32:36.390
Let's select the
subscription-based Defender.

00:32:36.390 --> 00:32:38.940
Let's configure it.

00:32:38.940 --> 00:32:40.650
And let's scroll down.

00:32:40.650 --> 00:32:44.820
What we're going to do here is
connect it to our subscription.

00:32:44.820 --> 00:32:48.180
All I need to do is select
a subscription, connect it,

00:32:48.180 --> 00:32:50.010
and optionally choose
whether we want

00:32:50.010 --> 00:32:53.880
to enable bi-directional
sync between Sentinel

00:32:53.880 --> 00:32:56.100
